WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.650
JENNY BROWN: This
week, we're talking

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:06.050
about the What-If Tool, AI
codelabs, and database basics.

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:08.880
I'm Jenny Brown, and welcome
to "This Week in Cloud," where

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.300
we serve you the
lowest latency news.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.340
Last year, we introduced
the What-If Tool

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:15.940
for TensorFlow models.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:19.620
And now, we're making it
available on AI Platform too.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.780
You can use the What-If
Tool to gain a deeper

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:25.470
understanding of how your models
work under different scenarios.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.440
Grab the code from the
post to get started.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.140
Or, for a little more
guidance, this codelab

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.940
is a great place to start.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.790
In it, you'll learn exactly
how to train models in Cloud AI

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:38.820
Platform, generate
predictions, and of course,

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.700
compare different models
using the What-If Tool.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.360
Take an hour this week to
upgrade your modeling skills.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.150
For a deeper dive,
@GoogleAI has published

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.220
a bunch of free guides
on GitHub to help you

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.710
learn about Keras and OpenCV.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:55.350
We think it's the perfect
way for software engineers

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:59.550
who want a straight path
with no math to #ML.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.890
So if you want a
comprehensive set of resources

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.379
and don't want to look
in multiple places,

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:07.830
@GoogleAI's collection
is definitely for you.

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:09.600
If you've ever
thought about running

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.910
a database in the
cloud, this article

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:13.680
is a great place to start.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.920
You'll get a roundup of recently
published database solutions

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.970
from deploying the common IBM
Db2 in the cloud to migrating

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.430
an existing database to GCP.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:26.160
There's even tips on
backing up your database

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.290
and how to add
features like tracing.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:29.730
Go check it out.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:32.010
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:34.870
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:36.550
Have any thoughts or ideas?

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:38.010
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.280
We'll see you in the cloud.

